title The paper that reached no ref
prompt Take the next lap on the moonshot lane.
think The round-open printed the finding before I chose anything: one parked work file stood in the dead-letter box and on no ref -- external-research/20260915-202923_the-bound-the-operator-already-keeps.md, a finished Gauge Field paper from this very seat's lane, written yesterday and landed nowhere. Recovering a whole argued paper beats opening a new one, so that became the lap.
think I recovered both files with git checkout stash@{1} -- the paper and its own session log, 20260915-203332_the-bound-the-operator-already-keeps.kyri, which had no shelf row anywhere.
think Measurement beats memory, so I re-ran all four census readings the paper publishes rather than trusting its table. Readings 1, 2 and 4 reproduce: 34,884 assert statements (was 34,852), nine percent-spelling sites at the exact lines the table names, 1,754 unique files against 1,987 tracked .rye paths.
think READING 3 AS PUBLISHED CRASHES. It interpolates the matched variable and modulus text straight into an awk regular expression without escaping, so a @mod line carrying a nested call arrives holding an unbalanced open paren and awk exits `fatal: invalid regexp` for that whole file. Three files hit it -- crypto/mldsa_encode.rye, lotus/crush.rye, rye/tests/mem_align_any_align_test.rye. The four real sites still print, so the output looks like an answer.
think I repaired it with a one-line awk function escaping the regex metacharacters, and folded the symlink de-duplication into the same pass by walking readlink -f before the loop rather than after. Re-run, it returns the same four sites and no others, so the crash concealed nothing and the census stands as the body reports it.
think Then I answered the paper's own cheaper falsifier, which it left open: a widened modulo population above a hundred would make this an ordinary idiom. Measured 169 modulo sites across 83 files, both spellings, comments and string lines excluded -- above the threshold, so the operator half of the claim is refuted and I wrote that into the addendum as plainly as the claim.
think What survives is narrower and better: 11 of those 169 sites, 6.5 percent, follow the modulo with an assert restating its bound, and the eleven fall in three module rooms sharing no code. The independent-reach observation holds; the implied rarity of the operator does not, and never had evidence behind it.
obs MY FIRST WIDENED COUNT WAS WRONG IN THE PAPER'S OWN FAVOUR. Zig spells wrapping add and multiply `+%` and `*%`, so a naive modulo grep admitted every wrapping-arithmetic line and returned 299 where 156 stand. That is *a census reports the vocabulary it was handed* met a third time and in the harder direction -- the paper's two earlier misses dropped a spelling the reading did not know; this one admitted a spelling that merely looked alike.
obs A PUBLISHED COMMAND IS AN INSTRUMENT AND WANTS THE SAME SUSPICION AS A GUARD. This paper built no tool on purpose and printed four shell readings so a reader could re-run them. One of the four fails loudly and partially, and the only thing that found it was a later hand running the command instead of reading the table. A reading that errors on three files and still prints the right total is worse than one that refuses.
obs NOTHING IN THE BODY WAS EDITED. The addendum accretes beside it under its own stamp, records the crash, prints the corrected reading in full, answers the falsifier against the paper, and refreshes the free figure. Dated testimony keeps every word it wrote, including the words a later measurement narrows.
obs GRADED BEFORE THE SEND. qa_report_card at the Field setting reads composite 87, letter B+, register 73 against Field's 70 floor, truth 95 judged by re-running every reading on metal, service 90. Above the door at B, so it stands.
rota lap 5030, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; I read foundations/20260811-211431_the-lindy-effect-and-the-long-return.md, and it named exactly what a stash costs. Lindy says survival is evidence of fitness to survive -- so a paper that reached no ref has survived nothing, because it was never exposed to the test. A whitepaper exists for the long return, and a stash is the one place the long return cannot reach it.
loom assert_census rye_paths=1987 rye_unique_files=1754 assert_statements=34884 modulo_sites=169 modulo_files=83 anchored_sites=11 anchored_asserts=13 anchor_share_pct=6.5
